266
00:08:50.921 --> 00:08:53.257
Like, everything that you're about

267
00:08:53.257 --> 00:08:55.883
indicates that this is the type of of environment

268
00:08:55.883 --> 00:08:59.901
that you would choose to live in and raise your children in.

269
00:08:59.901 --> 00:09:02.476
Yeah, I think originally, we left London,

270
00:09:02.476 --> 00:09:06.328
Katarina and I left London when Lola was a newborn, really.

271
00:09:06.328 --> 00:09:08.607
Like nine months I guess.

272
00:09:08.607 --> 00:09:10.438
And we were just assessing at that point,

273
00:09:10.438 --> 00:09:11.632
where would we want to be?

274
00:09:11.632 --> 00:09:14.737
And we moved out of London and then we moved into,

275
00:09:14.737 --> 00:09:16.205
well, first of all we went to my parents'

276
00:09:16.205 --> 00:09:17.559
and we were living with my parents for a period of time

277
00:09:17.559 --> 00:09:18.392
for six months.

278
00:09:18.392 --> 00:09:20.024
Then we moved into Windsor

279
00:09:20.024 --> 00:09:21.903
and we lived in this lovely little community,

280
00:09:21.903 --> 00:09:23.624
like picket fences and cottages

281
00:09:23.624 --> 00:09:27.183
and a big willow tree in the square, no cars.

282
00:09:27.183 --> 00:09:29.097
But certain things weren't quite aligning.

283
00:09:29.097 --> 00:09:32.000
It was a community, but it wasn't aligned with say,

284
00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:33.107
our values, right?

285
00:09:33.107 --> 00:09:36.196
So food was at question and, you know,

286
00:09:36.196 --> 00:09:37.866
just certain behaviors were at question

287
00:09:37.866 --> 00:09:39.007
and weren't quite where we were

288
00:09:39.007 --> 00:09:41.156
and they were on a different path, you know?

289
00:09:41.156 --> 00:09:41.989
Yeah.

290
00:09:41.989 --> 00:09:43.082
It's no judgment of where people were,

291
00:09:43.082 --> 00:09:44.935
it was just their path, right?

292
00:09:44.935 --> 00:09:45.854
And so we at that point were like,

293
00:09:45.854 --> 00:09:47.809
"well, where do we want to be?"

294
00:09:47.809 --> 00:09:50.594
And then friends that I was with

295
00:09:50.594 --> 00:09:52.490
and bouncing in and out of London with

296
00:09:52.490 --> 00:09:55.635
were like, "You should come to Ibiza. Have a look at Ibiza."

297
00:09:55.635 --> 00:09:56.468
Yeah.

298
00:09:56.468 --> 00:09:57.301
So next thing we moved to Ibiza

299
00:09:57.301 --> 00:09:59.325
and that kind of was a transition.

300
00:09:59.325 --> 00:10:01.401
It never felt like it was gonna become home,

301
00:10:01.401 --> 00:10:03.363
but what it allowed us to do

302
00:10:03.363 --> 00:10:05.485
is pluck ourselves out of that community,

303
00:10:05.485 --> 00:10:08.305
immerse in a community that we're very much aligned.

304
00:10:08.305 --> 00:10:10.049
You know, suddenly it was, you know,

305
00:10:10.049 --> 00:10:12.323
cacao ceremonies, voice-awakening work,

306
00:10:12.323 --> 00:10:13.526
and plant medicines-

307
00:10:13.526 --> 00:10:14.359
Yeah.

308
00:10:14.359 --> 00:10:15.192
...and ecstatic dance,

309
00:10:15.192 --> 00:10:16.025
and all this stuff.

310
00:10:16.025 --> 00:10:17.894
And being able to walk around butt naked and bare foot

311
00:10:17.894 --> 00:10:19.137
pretty much all day, right?

312
00:10:19.137 --> 00:10:19.970
(Rich laughs)

313
00:10:19.970 --> 00:10:22.296
And we had Lola, Millie, and Tallulah

314
00:10:22.296 --> 00:10:23.932
and we were pretty much naked the whole time.

315
00:10:23.932 --> 00:10:24.765
It would be,

316
00:10:24.765 --> 00:10:26.522
"come on kids, let's get to the beach."

317
00:10:26.522 --> 00:10:29.130
And the kids would just jump into a big old Cherokee

318
00:10:29.130 --> 00:10:29.963
at that stage.

319
00:10:29.963 --> 00:10:32.322
Like battered up old thing that didn't really...

320
00:10:32.322 --> 00:10:33.155
no one cared really.

321
00:10:33.155 --> 00:10:34.774
And the kids were in the back of that,

322
00:10:34.774 --> 00:10:36.602
climbing all over the seats naked.

323
00:10:36.602 --> 00:10:38.103
And then you get to the beach naked,

324
00:10:38.103 --> 00:10:39.388
get back in the car naked,

325
00:10:39.388 --> 00:10:40.590
arrive back at the house naked.

326
00:10:40.590 --> 00:10:41.423
(Rich laughs)

327
00:10:41.423 --> 00:10:42.840
And that was life, right?

328
00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:45.891
But unfortunately, I was still having to fly in and out.

329
00:10:45.891 --> 00:10:48.468
So I'd have to come into London and then back out.

330
00:10:48.468 --> 00:10:50.597
Had it been now, everything would've been online.

331
00:10:50.597 --> 00:10:51.901
It would've been very different for me.

332
00:10:51.901 --> 00:10:55.404
I could have, you know, we would probably still be there,

333
00:10:55.404 --> 00:10:57.757
but there's this notion that the island's like

334
00:10:57.757 --> 00:10:58.986
an island of transformation.

335
00:10:58.986 --> 00:11:02.019
And when you're done, you're ready to leave,

336
00:11:02.019 --> 00:11:02.978
it kind of spits you out.

337
00:11:02.978 --> 00:11:03.811
Pushes you away.

338
00:11:03.811 --> 00:11:04.644
Yeah!

339
00:11:04.644 --> 00:11:05.746
It kind of felt that way.

340
00:11:05.746 --> 00:11:08.233
So we were coming back on Christmas,

341
00:11:08.233 --> 00:11:10.191
it's a bit repetitive now, but Katarina had said,

342
00:11:10.191 --> 00:11:12.215
"look, you know, I think we need to go back

343
00:11:12.215 --> 00:11:13.893
and you've given us an amazing time here.

344
00:11:13.893 --> 00:11:15.593
It's been incredible,

345
00:11:15.593 --> 00:11:18.968
but it's your time now, let's get this book done."

346
00:11:18.968 --> 00:11:19.801
Mm-hmm.

347
00:11:19.801 --> 00:11:21.337
It was kind of... that was the push.

348
00:11:21.337 --> 00:11:22.969
Yeah, and to get a book out,

349
00:11:22.969 --> 00:11:24.186
you have to participate in the world.

350
00:11:24.186 --> 00:11:27.307
You can't opt out of modern western society

351
00:11:27.307 --> 00:11:30.639
and expect anybody to be interested in your book, right?

352
00:11:30.639 --> 00:11:34.487
So you have to remain in contact to some extent with London

353
00:11:34.487 --> 00:11:35.320
or urban centers

354
00:11:35.320 --> 00:11:37.536
in order to do that, which you did.

355
00:11:37.536 --> 00:11:41.198
But I think it brings up a central point that's at the core

356
00:11:41.198 --> 00:11:42.785
of all of your work, which is like,

357
00:11:42.785 --> 00:11:47.247
how do you live this more naturalistic lifestyle,

358
00:11:47.247 --> 00:11:49.540
more symbiotic with, you know,

359
00:11:49.540 --> 00:11:53.164
your natural biological rhythms and those of the planet

360
00:11:53.164 --> 00:11:55.771
while also, you know, existing in the world?

361
00:11:55.771 --> 00:11:57.128
Because I think a lot of people,

362
00:11:57.128 --> 00:11:58.438
it's easy to look at you and go,

363
00:11:58.438 --> 00:11:59.804
"oh, he's the barefoot freak

364
00:11:59.804 --> 00:12:00.950
and he doesn't have any furniture

365
00:12:00.950 --> 00:12:02.639
and his kids are running around naked

366
00:12:02.639 --> 00:12:03.500
and he's, you know,

367
00:12:03.500 --> 00:12:04.811
doing cacao ceremonies."

368
00:12:04.811 --> 00:12:06.007
Like, that's not my life.

369
00:12:06.007 --> 00:12:08.045
Like, that's not... I don't...

370
00:12:08.045 --> 00:12:10.545
I have trouble like accessing,

371
00:12:11.727 --> 00:12:12.560
you know,

372
00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:16.543
what aspect of that is applicable to like how I'm living?

373
00:12:16.543 --> 00:12:17.654
And relatable, right?

374
00:12:17.654 --> 00:12:18.487
Unless I completely...

375
00:12:18.487 --> 00:12:19.651
Yeah, or relatable, right?

376
00:12:19.651 --> 00:12:21.728
Like, yeah, he's squatting, that's cool.

377
00:12:21.728 --> 00:12:22.561
Like am I gonna squat?

378
00:12:22.561 --> 00:12:26.137
I don't know, maybe, but how do I connect with this?

379
00:12:26.137 --> 00:12:29.605
And I think really the power of "Be More Human"

380
00:12:29.605 --> 00:12:32.480
is this edict that you repeat, you know,

381
00:12:32.480 --> 00:12:33.805
over the course of the many pages,

382
00:12:33.805 --> 00:12:34.638
which is like,

383
00:12:34.638 --> 00:12:38.905
this is not about like being a Luddite per se or opting out.

384
00:12:38.905 --> 00:12:40.315
It's about trying to, you know,

385
00:12:40.315 --> 00:12:41.928
find a rhythm that works for you

386
00:12:41.928 --> 00:12:44.794
where you can kind of take from this wisdom

387
00:12:44.794 --> 00:12:47.109
and build these habits and practices into your life

388
00:12:47.109 --> 00:12:49.539
without completely disrupting the way that you live.

389
00:12:49.539 --> 00:12:51.424
Yeah. I think, and again,

390
00:12:51.424 --> 00:12:55.150
it's walking that path and not just talking the path, right?

391
00:12:55.150 --> 00:12:55.983
So we...

392
00:12:57.620 --> 00:12:59.333
I think if it wasn't for lockdown,

393
00:12:59.333 --> 00:13:02.114
we'd have that really nice lifestyle in London, you know?

394
00:13:02.114 --> 00:13:02.965
Hampstead was-

395
00:13:02.965 --> 00:13:03.978
It's pretty great.

396
00:13:03.978 --> 00:13:05.818
Oh man, we had this amazing life.

397
00:13:05.818 --> 00:13:10.276
And again, you could have that nature immersion and except,

398
00:13:10.276 --> 00:13:12.803
you know, and, and throughout the book I also put this,

399
00:13:12.803 --> 00:13:14.327
you know, we can't all live in nature,

400
00:13:14.327 --> 00:13:15.327
but it doesn't mean we can't live naturally

401
00:13:15.327 --> 00:13:16.827
and we should be able to

402
00:13:16.827 --> 00:13:19.107
get our needs met in every environment.

403
00:13:19.107 --> 00:13:21.340
That's the point. It's just more often than not, we,

404
00:13:21.340 --> 00:13:23.164
here in the re-wilding circles at least,

405
00:13:23.164 --> 00:13:25.869
it's like this demonizing kind of zoo,

406
00:13:25.869 --> 00:13:26.945
the human zoo.

407
00:13:26.945 --> 00:13:29.292
Actually, it's not about demonizing the city life.

408
00:13:29.292 --> 00:13:30.836
It's about just dismantling,

409
00:13:30.836 --> 00:13:33.949
deconstructing ways of living that aren't really serving us

410
00:13:33.949 --> 00:13:35.015
in those environments.

411
00:13:35.015 --> 00:13:35.848
And then reconnecting to ways of living that

412
00:13:35.848 --> 00:13:39.322
of course are more in sync with our human biology-

413
00:13:39.322 --> 00:13:40.166
Mm-hmm.

414
00:13:40.166 --> 00:13:42.450
...like how we can move within everyday spaces.

415
00:13:42.450 --> 00:13:43.283
You know,

416
00:13:43.283 --> 00:13:44.534
if we looked at re-wilding that way,

417
00:13:44.534 --> 00:13:45.367
in the sense that I do,

418
00:13:45.367 --> 00:13:46.809
it's like you can re-wild your movement,

419
00:13:46.809 --> 00:13:47.642
your gut,

420
00:13:47.642 --> 00:13:48.475
your sleep,

421
00:13:48.475 --> 00:13:51.803
you know, your everyday behaviors in those experiences.

422
00:13:51.803 --> 00:13:56.116
And I think that for me has been the message, really.

423
00:13:56.116 --> 00:13:59.194
And then it's much more relatable. You know?

424
00:13:59.194 --> 00:14:01.446
I've lived in cities, I get it.

425
00:14:01.446 --> 00:14:04.908
And I also get what it is to be a papa to four kids

426
00:14:04.908 --> 00:14:06.362
and have businesses in London, you know?

427
00:14:06.362 --> 00:14:07.195
Sure.

428
00:14:07.195 --> 00:14:09.142
You know, and still manage to carve out time

429
00:14:09.142 --> 00:14:10.633
to become an endurance athlete.

430
00:14:10.633 --> 00:14:12.967
So I'm a hundred percent aligned, right?

431
00:14:12.967 --> 00:14:13.800
Yeah.

432
00:14:13.800 --> 00:14:15.234
I live it and I walk it,

433
00:14:15.234 --> 00:14:19.206
but also I have clients from all different backgrounds,

434
00:14:19.206 --> 00:14:20.462
all different demographics, right?

435
00:14:20.462 --> 00:14:22.441
From again, students through to billionaires.

436
00:14:22.441 --> 00:14:24.694
It's always the same message, right?

437
00:14:24.694 --> 00:14:28.324
My favorite is the example of the elder, um,

438
00:14:28.324 --> 00:14:30.424
he's an Indian gentleman, in the book

439
00:14:30.424 --> 00:14:31.753
and you kind of walk through

440
00:14:31.753 --> 00:14:34.345
like how you helped him transition his life

441
00:14:34.345 --> 00:14:39.071
and how he kind of lived it hour by hour as an urbanite.

442
00:14:39.071 --> 00:14:39.904
Yeah. So there's-

443
00:14:39.904 --> 00:14:40.741
What was that guy's name?

444
00:14:40.741 --> 00:14:42.395
So there's Yehudi in the book.

445
00:14:42.395 --> 00:14:43.662
Are we discussing Yehudi in this?

446
00:14:43.662 --> 00:14:44.495
Yeah.

447
00:14:44.495 --> 00:14:45.409
Yehudi's a Jewish guy.

448
00:14:45.409 --> 00:14:46.736
Oh he's Jewish.

449
00:14:46.736 --> 00:14:47.879
Yeah.

450
00:14:47.879 --> 00:14:50.673
He was... basically brought natural birthing

451
00:14:50.673 --> 00:14:51.802
really into the U.K.

452
00:14:51.802 --> 00:14:54.502
So if anyone's re-wilding natural birth in the U.K.,

453
00:14:54.502 --> 00:14:55.335
it's Yehudi.

454
00:14:55.335 --> 00:14:58.540
And he was ostracized at that time when he brought it in.

455
00:14:58.540 --> 00:15:00.424
You know, there's amazing stories from him.

456
00:15:00.424 --> 00:15:04.373
But he originally came to me wanting to learn how to walk,

457
00:15:04.373 --> 00:15:05.829
you know,

458
00:15:05.829 --> 00:15:07.293
and he was stooped like

459
00:15:07.293 --> 00:15:08.719
he was 72 at the time.

460
00:15:08.719 --> 00:15:11.524
So it was like what our template perhaps might have been

461
00:15:11.524 --> 00:15:13.234
of a 72 year old. Right?

462
00:15:13.234 --> 00:15:15.668
Stooped and just wanting to learn,

463
00:15:15.668 --> 00:15:17.311
how humble just to come in and say,

464
00:15:17.311 --> 00:15:18.371
"I want to learn how to walk."

465
00:15:18.371 --> 00:15:21.499
So I said, "Okay, let's just hop you up on a treadmill.

466
00:15:21.499 --> 00:15:22.332
I'll record you."

467
00:15:22.332 --> 00:15:25.458
First stage is you need to see what it is to walk,

468
00:15:25.458 --> 00:15:26.694
what it, looks like, right?

469
00:15:26.694 --> 00:15:27.527
Where you're at.

470
00:15:27.527 --> 00:15:29.332
Otherwise you're just kind of this...

471
00:15:29.332 --> 00:15:32.302
there's incompetence there subconscious incompetence

472
00:15:32.302 --> 00:15:33.135
until you drag it out.

473
00:15:33.135 --> 00:15:34.194
And then he's like very aware

474
00:15:34.194 --> 00:15:36.343
that it's conscious incompetence.

475
00:15:36.343 --> 00:15:38.863
So he could see the stoop and it was like,

476
00:15:38.863 --> 00:15:40.360
okay, let's go through these processes.

477
00:15:40.360 --> 00:15:42.609
And so the start of it was getting him back on the ground,

478
00:15:42.609 --> 00:15:43.939
just interacting with the ground.

479
00:15:43.939 --> 00:15:46.682
So like when we saw this guy without furniture,

480
00:15:46.682 --> 00:15:49.314
Yehudi became the guy without certain parts of furniture

481
00:15:49.314 --> 00:15:51.759
in his home, but the age of 72.

482
00:15:51.759 --> 00:15:54.698
And I think the thing with age also,

483
00:15:54.698 --> 00:15:58.203
because we have more patience in a way, you know?

484
00:15:58.203 --> 00:15:59.820
Like he was really patient with it

485
00:15:59.820 --> 00:16:01.785
and could allow things to grow and open up

486
00:16:01.785 --> 00:16:03.161
and saw that it was a long game.

487
00:16:03.161 --> 00:16:03.994
Mm-hmm.

488
00:16:03.994 --> 00:16:05.428
You know that's what he grasped

489
00:16:05.428 --> 00:16:06.387
from the very beginning.

490
00:16:06.387 --> 00:16:08.136
So I had him interacting with the ground

491
00:16:08.136 --> 00:16:09.305
and re-wilding his feet,

492
00:16:09.305 --> 00:16:11.504
then re-wilding his footwear.

493
00:16:11.504 --> 00:16:13.787
And there's amazing studies around there with footwear.

494
00:16:13.787 --> 00:16:16.150
you know, like Kris D'Aouût, University of Liverpool.

495
00:16:16.150 --> 00:16:18.808
60% of foot strength is lost.

496
00:16:18.808 --> 00:16:21.591
Well, they put people back in barefoot shoes

497
00:16:21.591 --> 00:16:23.932
or walked around barefoot- it's Vivobarefoot in this case.

498
00:16:23.932 --> 00:16:27.908
Six months of transitioning back into barefoot footwear,

499
00:16:27.908 --> 00:16:30.693
they found a 60% increase in foot strength,

500
00:16:30.693 --> 00:16:32.559
a 40% increase in balance.

501
00:16:32.559 --> 00:16:33.649
So for someone like Yehudi,

502
00:16:33.649 --> 00:16:34.482
it's like, wow, okay,

503
00:16:34.482 --> 00:16:36.211
not just improving is the way that he walks.

504
00:16:36.211 --> 00:16:37.651
It would be the way that he stands.

505
00:16:37.651 --> 00:16:38.484
Right.

506
00:16:38.484 --> 00:16:39.317
It'd be the way he squats

507
00:16:39.317 --> 00:16:42.708
or we could then ramp things up and get him into running.

508
00:16:42.708 --> 00:16:44.849
So then that became movement for him on the ground.

509
00:16:44.849 --> 00:16:48.272
So even his work set-up became a standing desk

510
00:16:48.272 --> 00:16:49.688
to squatting desks, you know.

511
00:16:49.688 --> 00:16:50.521
I mean incredible.

512
00:16:50.521 --> 00:16:52.014
And then we had a tray beneath him

513
00:16:52.014 --> 00:16:53.156
that would have stones in it

514
00:16:53.156 --> 00:16:54.549
so he could have his feet,

515
00:16:54.549 --> 00:16:56.073
rather than being on a flat linear surface-

516
00:16:56.073 --> 00:16:56.906
Right. Mm-hmm.

517
00:16:56.906 --> 00:16:58.053
He's now interacting with that kind of...

518
00:16:58.053 --> 00:16:59.456
those realms as well.

519
00:16:59.456 --> 00:17:00.357
Very nourishing.

520
00:17:00.357 --> 00:17:01.190
Yeah.

521
00:17:01.190 --> 00:17:02.023
And then eventually over time,

522
00:17:02.023 --> 00:17:02.856
come hanging practices,

523
00:17:02.856 --> 00:17:04.184
lifting practices.

524
00:17:04.184 --> 00:17:06.196
And then I discuss his commute in the book

525
00:17:06.196 --> 00:17:08.094
where, you know, he wakes up

526
00:17:08.094 --> 00:17:11.142
and first of all he's down to the next level of his house.

527
00:17:11.142 --> 00:17:14.123
He has a hang and then he answers his email standing

528
00:17:14.123 --> 00:17:15.834
then has his smoothie in the morning,

529
00:17:15.834 --> 00:17:17.110
then off he goes to the tube,

530
00:17:17.110 --> 00:17:18.152
he'd kiss his wife goodbye

