for as you go,

3705
01:50:51.690 --> 01:50:52.656
and then train.

3706
01:50:52.656 --> 01:50:54.986
I would at least get out of the chair,

3707
01:50:54.986 --> 01:50:56.165
like hold the back of the chair.

3708
01:50:56.165 --> 01:50:57.630
I call it a "posture squat."

3709
01:50:57.630 --> 01:50:59.904
And just allow your heels to pop up and drop into a squat.

3710
01:50:59.904 --> 01:51:00.737
A few little bounces,

3711
01:51:00.737 --> 01:51:01.570
stand up,

3712
01:51:01.570 --> 01:51:02.403
do a few of those-

3713
01:51:02.403 --> 01:51:03.236
Mm-hmm.

3714
01:51:03.236 --> 01:51:04.124
...and then walk off. So-

3715
01:51:04.124 --> 01:51:06.726
Why is having the heel elevated so important?

3716
01:51:06.726 --> 01:51:08.128
I've heard you talk about that a lot.

3717
01:51:08.128 --> 01:51:11.027
Well, because again, you can focus on posture.

3718
01:51:11.027 --> 01:51:12.763
You can start to think about keeping the chest up

3719
01:51:12.763 --> 01:51:13.599
and the head up then.

3720
01:51:13.599 --> 01:51:15.494
Whereas if you were to get straight out of the chair

3721
01:51:15.494 --> 01:51:16.327
and squat down

3722
01:51:16.327 --> 01:51:18.518
when you've been locked up in the ankles and the hips

3723
01:51:18.518 --> 01:51:19.351
more often than not

3724
01:51:19.351 --> 01:51:21.559
the back ends up really rounded within that conversation.

3725
01:51:21.559 --> 01:51:22.548
So it helps with the...

3726
01:51:22.548 --> 01:51:24.013
that's why I call it a "posture squat."

3727
01:51:24.013 --> 01:51:24.846
Yeah.

3728
01:51:24.846 --> 01:51:25.679
It helps enhance the posture.

3729
01:51:25.679 --> 01:51:26.512
Yeah.

3730
01:51:26.512 --> 01:51:27.345
Keeps the head up and the chest up.

3731
01:51:27.345 --> 01:51:30.970
For some, it's the fact that the ankle is...

3732
01:51:30.970 --> 01:51:33.162
there isn't enough dorsiflexion in the ankle anyway

3733
01:51:33.162 --> 01:51:34.609
to achieve the squat.

3734
01:51:34.609 --> 01:51:35.599
So then what happens

3735
01:51:35.599 --> 01:51:37.207
is we try and achieve a flatfooted squat.

3736
01:51:37.207 --> 01:51:38.040
We end up over-pronated

3737
01:51:38.040 --> 01:51:40.290
in the ankle, the knees, and the hips.

3738
01:51:40.290 --> 01:51:42.381
And that, again, isn't the best foundation

3739
01:51:42.381 --> 01:51:45.029
for standing, walking, running, or jumping or balancing.

3740
01:51:45.029 --> 01:51:45.862
Yeah.

3741
01:51:45.862 --> 01:51:46.695
So in that case,

3742
01:51:46.695 --> 01:51:48.360
I would then put a wedge behind the heel

3743
01:51:48.360 --> 01:51:51.181
and there's... you can buy cork wedges for squatting now.

3744
01:51:51.181 --> 01:51:52.034
So you have a thick edge

3745
01:51:52.034 --> 01:51:54.119
and it works its way down to a thin edge.

3746
01:51:54.119 --> 01:51:55.160
And so to start, you could go

3747
01:51:55.160 --> 01:51:55.993
all right,

3748
01:51:55.993 --> 01:51:56.826
set a timer...

3749
01:51:56.826 --> 01:51:58.365
you know, Portal's kind of 30 minute squat challenge,

3750
01:51:58.365 --> 01:51:59.198
right?

3751
01:51:59.198 --> 01:52:00.723
Put the thick raise behind the heel

3752
01:52:00.723 --> 01:52:03.189
30 minutes a day for a number of days, right?

3753
01:52:03.189 --> 01:52:04.705
Can I start to bring my heel down

3754
01:52:04.705 --> 01:52:05.826
to the thinner edge over time

3755
01:52:05.826 --> 01:52:08.386
and then eventually on the thin edge

3756
01:52:08.386 --> 01:52:09.219
and then

3757
01:52:09.219 --> 01:52:10.410
eventually to the ground?

3758
01:52:10.410 --> 01:52:13.029
But that's step by step by step by step.

3759
01:52:13.029 --> 01:52:14.316
Allowing that physiology

3760
01:52:14.316 --> 01:52:17.806
and the mind to make the micro adjustments that are needed.

3761
01:52:17.806 --> 01:52:19.824
Otherwise we go straight into it.

3762
01:52:19.824 --> 01:52:21.406
Force the angle into a position,

3763
01:52:21.406 --> 01:52:22.403
the knee into a position,

3764
01:52:22.403 --> 01:52:23.236
hip into a position.

3765
01:52:23.236 --> 01:52:24.871
Do that for 30 days.

3766
01:52:24.871 --> 01:52:27.853
Or do the squat challenge for 30 days.

3767
01:52:27.853 --> 01:52:30.064
And then what have you learned from that?

3768
01:52:30.064 --> 01:52:33.867
You've learned to create a really unstable foundation,

3769
01:52:33.867 --> 01:52:34.880
really, for how you stand.

3770
01:52:34.880 --> 01:52:35.713
Mm-hmm.

3771
01:52:35.713 --> 01:52:38.692
One of the things that I've become hyper aware of

3772
01:52:38.692 --> 01:52:40.787
that I've noticed more and more

3773
01:52:40.787 --> 01:52:42.245
as I'm paying more attention

3774
01:52:42.245 --> 01:52:46.039
to what's going on in my lower back, et cetera,

3775
01:52:46.039 --> 01:52:48.583
is a loss of balance.

3776
01:52:48.583 --> 01:52:52.012
Like a lack of balance that I used to have.

3777
01:52:52.012 --> 01:52:53.450
Like if I stand on one foot...

3778
01:52:53.450 --> 01:52:54.727
like I used to be able to do

3779
01:52:54.727 --> 01:52:56.831
all kinds of crazy yoga postures and,

3780
01:52:56.831 --> 01:52:57.664
you know,

3781
01:52:57.664 --> 01:52:59.855
balanced exercises.

3782
01:52:59.855 --> 01:53:01.005
And recently,

3783
01:53:01.005 --> 01:53:02.624
I was like,

3784
01:53:02.624 --> 01:53:06.700
"wow, I can't even hold my posture stable

3785
01:53:06.700 --> 01:53:07.533
on one leg,

3786
01:53:07.533 --> 01:53:09.708
let alone like lift the other leg

3787
01:53:09.708 --> 01:53:11.791
or do anything compound."

3788
01:53:12.804 --> 01:53:14.595
Like just basic stuff.

3789
01:53:14.595 --> 01:53:18.823
Like putting on a sock while I'm standing on the other foot,

3790
01:53:18.823 --> 01:53:20.221
I find myself teetering

3791
01:53:20.221 --> 01:53:21.328
and I'm thinking,

3792
01:53:21.328 --> 01:53:23.899
is this because I'm getting older?

3793
01:53:23.899 --> 01:53:24.732
No!

3794
01:53:24.732 --> 01:53:27.412
It's because you've lost that

3795
01:53:27.412 --> 01:53:29.329
foundation of stability

3796
01:53:30.807 --> 01:53:34.594
and sort of dispersed strength in all the right places

3797
01:53:34.594 --> 01:53:37.216
to hold your body upwardly stable.

3798
01:53:37.216 --> 01:53:38.049
Yeah.

3799
01:53:38.049 --> 01:53:40.718
So, I could go, "what would the reboot be for that?"

3800
01:53:40.718 --> 01:53:44.336
There's a guy called Tom I'm working with, he's like 77.

3801
01:53:44.336 --> 01:53:46.040
He plays a lot of tennis,

3802
01:53:46.040 --> 01:53:48.371
And we've been working on footwork and balance techniques

3803
01:53:48.371 --> 01:53:49.486
rather than tennis

3804
01:53:49.486 --> 01:53:51.255
because ultimately that is what he needs-

3805
01:53:51.255 --> 01:53:52.088
Yeah.

3806
01:53:52.088 --> 01:53:52.987
...to play tennis, right?

3807
01:53:52.987 --> 01:53:54.251
Otherwise just relying on whipping his arm out

3808
01:53:54.251 --> 01:53:55.523
and not getting to the ball in time

3809
01:53:55.523 --> 01:53:57.260
or creating an injury through that.

3810
01:53:57.260 --> 01:54:00.025
And it starts off being barefoot again,

3811
01:54:00.025 --> 01:54:00.896
lifting your head,

3812
01:54:00.896 --> 01:54:01.729
your chest

3813
01:54:01.729 --> 01:54:02.562
and your pelvis,

3814
01:54:02.562 --> 01:54:03.395
everything is stacked

3815
01:54:03.395 --> 01:54:04.228
and being barefoot.

3816
01:54:04.228 --> 01:54:06.701
The reason barefoot is that's your foundation again.

3817
01:54:06.701 --> 01:54:08.092
You make a reconnection to that.

3818
01:54:08.092 --> 01:54:09.541
So he starts just eyes closed

3819
01:54:09.541 --> 01:54:12.556
and feeling into the big toes, the smaller toes,

3820
01:54:12.556 --> 01:54:15.130
the big pads behind them that we call the ball of the foot,

3821
01:54:15.130 --> 01:54:16.634
then the outer edge

3822
01:54:16.634 --> 01:54:17.716
and then the heel.

3823
01:54:17.716 --> 01:54:18.624
'Cause you can't...

3824
01:54:18.624 --> 01:54:20.419
the arch isn't tangible, right?

3825
01:54:20.419 --> 01:54:21.252
Because there's no...

3826
01:54:21.252 --> 01:54:23.048
you can't experience gravity through an arch.

3827
01:54:23.048 --> 01:54:23.881
Mm-hmm.

3828
01:54:23.881 --> 01:54:25.309
Well your lateral arch you can, sorry,

3829
01:54:25.309 --> 01:54:26.298
but think about the pads

3830
01:54:26.298 --> 01:54:28.100
where you're making contact to the ground

3831
01:54:28.100 --> 01:54:29.740
and really tune into them, right?

3832
01:54:29.740 --> 01:54:31.523
Heighten your awareness of them.

3833
01:54:31.523 --> 01:54:33.288
And you take your visual state out

3834
01:54:33.288 --> 01:54:35.165
so it enhances your proprioceptive state

3835
01:54:35.165 --> 01:54:36.320
and your vestibular,

3836
01:54:36.320 --> 01:54:37.153
your inner ear, within that communication,

3837
01:54:37.153 --> 01:54:39.673
which is like your somato system.

3838
01:54:39.673 --> 01:54:41.086
So that's based on visual,

3839
01:54:41.086 --> 01:54:41.919
vestibular,

3840
01:54:41.919 --> 01:54:43.178
and proprioceptive.

3841
01:54:43.178 --> 01:54:44.411
And you keep tuning in,

3842
01:54:44.411 --> 01:54:45.244
keep tuning in,

3843
01:54:45.244 --> 01:54:46.077
keep tuning in,

3844
01:54:46.077 --> 01:54:47.489
and start to then move segments.

3845
01:54:47.489 --> 01:54:48.619
So you try and work like a...

3846
01:54:48.619 --> 01:54:51.581
imagine like a broom handle from the top to your ankle.

3847
01:54:51.581 --> 01:54:53.257
And rather than moving with your head,

3848
01:54:53.257 --> 01:54:57.467
you move the whole pole like a metronome needle

3849
01:54:57.467 --> 01:54:58.316
and that's you.

3850
01:54:58.316 --> 01:55:01.576
But keeping your awareness in the base of support, the feet,

3851
01:55:01.576 --> 01:55:02.409
the whole time

3852
01:55:02.409 --> 01:55:03.254
and work with that

3853
01:55:03.254 --> 01:55:04.371
and then try and draw a circle

3854
01:55:04.371 --> 01:55:05.524
around that base of the support

3855
01:55:05.524 --> 01:55:07.660
without creating any breaks in that pole.

3856
01:55:07.660 --> 01:55:09.800
So the pelvis doesn't hinge or the back doesn't hinge

3857
01:55:09.800 --> 01:55:10.633
or the neck.

3858
01:55:10.633 --> 01:55:11.466
Hmmm.

3859
01:55:11.466 --> 01:55:12.468
It's one long thing working like this.

3860
01:55:12.468 --> 01:55:13.301
Yeah?

3861
01:55:13.301 --> 01:55:14.203
No camera here, you guys.

3862
01:55:14.203 --> 01:55:15.281
So you have to kind of work with me.

3863
01:55:15.281 --> 01:55:17.649
So it's like a pole being held from the top and the bottom.

3864
01:55:17.649 --> 01:55:18.493
The bottom is the anchor.

3865
01:55:18.493 --> 01:55:20.023
When you grab the pole from the top being your head

3866
01:55:20.023 --> 01:55:21.736
and you draw a circle.

3867
01:55:21.736 --> 01:55:23.394
That's the first stage and work on that.

3868
01:55:23.394 --> 01:55:26.218
And that'll start to bring your segments

3869
01:55:26.218 --> 01:55:28.041
or you're understanding how to move your segments

3870
01:55:28.041 --> 01:55:30.151
around your base of support.

3871
01:55:30.151 --> 01:55:32.603
Then I would go have your feet a little wider

3872
01:55:32.603 --> 01:55:34.265
and then work foe like tick-tocking.

3873
01:55:34.265 --> 01:55:35.989
So as you go over all your segments

3874
01:55:35.989 --> 01:55:36.892
with your head and your chest,

3875
01:55:36.892 --> 01:55:37.873
over your left side,

3876
01:55:37.873 --> 01:55:40.087
you think about lightening your right side

3877
01:55:40.087 --> 01:55:42.419
and increasing your perception of your left foot,

3878
01:55:42.419 --> 01:55:43.252
those pads.

3879
01:55:43.252 --> 01:55:44.976
And then you take your head and your chest and your pelvis

3880
01:55:44.976 --> 01:55:46.879
right over to your right side,

3881
01:55:46.879 --> 01:55:47.712
lift your left foot.

3882
01:55:47.712 --> 01:55:48.545
Mm-hmm.

3883
01:55:48.545 --> 01:55:49.765
And then eventually you go all the way over

3884
01:55:49.765 --> 01:55:50.685
and stack all your segments

3885
01:55:50.685 --> 01:55:53.098
and then lift the leg up and then you go back over

3886
01:55:53.098 --> 01:55:53.931
and you lift-

3887
01:55:53.931 --> 01:55:54.764
Right.

3888
01:55:54.764 --> 01:55:56.495
And so Tony's sort of teetering back and forth

3889
01:55:56.495 --> 01:55:57.328
based upon which leg the weight's on

3890
01:55:57.328 --> 01:55:58.161
Yeah.

3891
01:55:58.161 --> 01:55:59.407
And you're organizing all of those segments

3892
01:55:59.407 --> 01:56:00.317
above your base of support.

3893
01:56:00.317 --> 01:56:01.546
And you really keep tuning,

3894
01:56:01.546 --> 01:56:03.884
do not lose perception of the base of support.

3895
01:56:03.884 --> 01:56:04.717
What can happen is

3896
01:56:04.717 --> 01:56:06.954
we get so lost in the action of like what I'm doing

3897
01:56:06.954 --> 01:56:07.787
with the leg

3898
01:56:07.787 --> 01:56:09.187
that we take full focus away from

3899
01:56:09.187 --> 01:56:10.711
what we're using to balance on,

3900
01:56:10.711 --> 01:56:12.949
which is the base of support, our foot.

3901
01:56:12.949 --> 01:56:13.782
And then you can go from,

3902
01:56:13.782 --> 01:56:14.851
right now I'm on one leg

3903
01:56:14.851 --> 01:56:15.935
and then start to think about,

3904
01:56:15.935 --> 01:56:19.244
"right, can I draw a circle with this left leg?"

3905
01:56:19.244 --> 01:56:20.077
Right?

3906
01:56:20.077 --> 01:56:22.423
Whilst keeping my perception on here.

3907
01:56:22.423 --> 01:56:24.206
So the key then is to keep your awareness

3908
01:56:24.206 --> 01:56:26.301
but also be able to draw a circle with this leg.

3909
01:56:26.301 --> 01:56:27.134
Mm-hmm.

3910
01:56:27.134 --> 01:56:27.967
Like just from the hip,

3911
01:56:27.967 --> 01:56:29.138
not not your whole body,

3912
01:56:29.138 --> 01:56:30.361
or not from the foot alone or the knee.

3913
01:56:30.361 --> 01:56:31.423
It's from the hip to the ankle.

3914
01:56:31.423 --> 01:56:35.986
Proximal being the hip and the distal point being the foot.

3915
01:56:35.986 --> 01:56:36.939
And you draw a circle,

3916
01:56:36.939 --> 01:56:38.257
say 10 one way 10 the other.

3917
01:56:38.257 --> 01:56:39.827
Switch your weight over; 10 one 10 the other.

3918
01:56:39.827 --> 01:56:41.322
And that's the first stage.

3919
01:56:41.322 --> 01:56:45.103
That will make a huge shift already to your balance, right?

3920
01:56:45.103 --> 01:56:45.936
Mm-hmm.

3921
01:56:45.936 --> 01:56:47.508
Getting away from shoes

3922
01:56:47.508 --> 01:56:48.874
that are narrow in the toe box,

3923
01:56:48.874 --> 01:56:50.494
that compromise those 26 bones,

3924
01:56:50.494 --> 01:56:53.574
33 joints, and 100 muscles, tendons, and ligaments

3925
01:56:53.574 --> 01:56:55.417
and the proprioceptive feedback they give.

3926
01:56:55.417 --> 01:56:56.634
Getting away from cushioning

3927
01:56:56.634 --> 01:56:59.429
because the cushioning also dumbs down the awareness.

3928
01:56:59.429 --> 01:57:00.894
So that study is really important.

3929
01:57:00.894 --> 01:57:03.572
That one I mentioned earlier, Professor Kris D'Aouût

3930
01:57:03.572 --> 01:57:05.030
from the University of Liverpool,

3931
01:57:05.030 --> 01:57:06.948
that returning back to Vivobarefoot shoes,

3932
01:57:06.948 --> 01:57:09.725
all being barefoot for six months,

3933
01:57:09.725 --> 01:57:13.194
was a 60% increase in foot strength,

3934
01:57:13.194 --> 01:57:16.092
but also 40% increase in balance.

3935
01:57:16.092 --> 01:57:18.273
So that was without doing the drills.

3936
01:57:18.273 --> 01:57:20.383
You know, I'm giving you some exercises to go with it.

3937
01:57:20.383 --> 01:57:21.974
So the environment would be the foot.

3938
01:57:21.974 --> 01:57:23.694
This is like the practices

3939
01:57:23.694 --> 01:57:25.022
to add to that new environment

3940
01:57:25.022 --> 01:57:25.882
being nature

3941
01:57:25.882 --> 01:57:27.422
or footwear that's wide enough

3942
01:57:27.422 --> 01:57:29.606
to enable your feet to perform like feet.

3943
01:57:29.606 --> 01:57:31.473
For some that conversation's really hard

3944
01:57:31.473 --> 01:57:33.954
because they've become so desensitized to an environment.

3945
01:57:33.954 --> 01:57:37.310
The foot is quite rigid on another hard surface.

3946
01:57:37.310 --> 01:57:38.490
So what could we do?

3947
01:57:38.490 --> 01:57:42.353
I would maybe work on softer surfaces to begin with.

3948
01:57:42.353 --> 01:57:43.380
And then over time

3949
01:57:43.380 --> 01:57:45.971
the the feet will start to open up and behave.

3950
01:57:45.971 --> 01:57:48.744
And then, with that balancing work, then try hops.

3951
01:57:48.744 --> 01:57:50.588
So draw a line on the floor

3952
01:57:50.588 --> 01:57:52.003
and once you're on one foot,

3953
01:57:52.003 --> 01:57:53.579
try and hop from one side to the other

3954
01:57:53.579 --> 01:57:54.944
all the way along the line

3955
01:57:54.944 --> 01:57:56.032
and then hop back again.

3956
01:57:56.032 --> 01:57:58.301
But do it in the like the running shape.

3957
01:57:58.301 --> 01:57:59.360
You know what I say about running,

3958
01:57:59.360 --> 01:58:00.193
so the head is up-

3959
01:58:00.193 --> 01:58:01.026
Mm-hmm.

3960
01:58:01.026 --> 01:58:01.859
...the chest is up,

3961
01:58:01.859 --> 01:58:03.029
and the ankles pulled underneath you of the opposite leg.

3962
01:58:03.029 --> 01:58:03.862
Mm-hmm.

3963
01:58:03.862 --> 01:58:04.695
Have your hands up

3964
01:58:04.695 --> 01:58:05.820
and just go ding, ding, ding, ding, ding, ding.

3965
01:58:05.820 --> 01:58:06.653
And then back again.

3966
01:58:06.653 --> 01:58:07.486
Mm-hmm.

3967
01:58:07.486 --> 01:58:08.319
Yeah?

3968
01:58:08.319 --> 01:58:09.152
And then change legs.

3969
01:58:09.152 --> 01:58:09.985
Ding, ding, ding, ding.

3970
01:58:09.985 --> 01:58:10.818
You only need like the length of this room.

3971
01:58:10.818 --> 01:58:13.029
Or it could just be to begin with a meter at a time, right?

3972
01:58:13.029 --> 01:58:14.149
And then change legs.

3973
01:58:14.149 --> 01:58:14.982
Hop, hop, hop.

3974
01:58:14.982 --> 01:58:15.815
And then change legs.

3975
01:58:15.815 --> 01:58:16.648
Hop, hop, hop.

3976
01:58:16.648 --> 01:58:18.115
And then work with both feet together.

3977
01:58:18.115 --> 01:58:19.153
Hop, hop, hop, hop.

3978
01:58:19.153 --> 01:58:19.986
Yeah.

3979
01:58:19.986 --> 01:58:21.777
And play with kind of the elasticity

3980
01:58:21.777 --> 01:58:23.955
and the ground reaction work within that.

3981
01:58:23.955 --> 01:58:26.899
And that will reinforce when I need to be on

3982
01:58:26.899 --> 01:58:28.445
and when I need to be off.

3983
01:58:28.445 --> 01:58:30.066
So you start to get those natural responses

3984
01:58:30.066 --> 01:58:32.453
to when the glutes need to be on: when I land.

3985
01:58:32.453 --> 01:58:34.348
Yeah, and the glutes need to be on when I balance,

3986
01:58:34.348 --> 01:58:35.774
so you can bring all that work in.

3987
01:58:35.774 --> 01:58:36.722
Mm-hmm.

3988
01:58:36.722 --> 01:58:37.802
Then there's heckling,

3989
01:58:37.802 --> 01:58:38.635
Heckling,

3990
01:58:38.635 --> 01:58:40.307
you can work with another person with it

3991
01:58:40.307 --> 01:58:42.251
or you can play with yourself with this, right?

3992
01:58:42.251 --> 01:58:44.456
So you keep that same segments aligned

3993
01:58:44.456 --> 01:58:45.924
with the head up and the chest up,

3994
01:58:45.924 --> 01:58:49.133
head, chest, pelvis, stacked above your base of support.

3995
01:58:49.133 --> 01:58:51.412
Tune into the big toe, the smaller toes, the heel,

3996
01:58:51.412 --> 01:58:54.046
try and think two thirds of your weight being forward,

3997
01:58:54.046 --> 01:58:56.145
only a third remaining in the heel

3998
01:58:56.145 --> 01:58:57.836
with the other leg that's now off the ground

3999
01:58:57.836 --> 01:59:00.390
that we've managed to draw circles with and everything else.

4000
01:59:00.390 --> 01:59:03.947
Use that leg to try and throw you off balance.

4001
01:59:03.947 --> 01:59:06.752
So kick it around, thrash it around, whatever it is.

4002
01:59:06.752 --> 01:59:09.051
And don't worry about what head and chest is doing.

4003
01:59:09.051 --> 01:59:11.599
The objective though is not to put that foot down.

4004
01:59:11.599 --> 01:59:14.155
And even if that means hopping or jumping around the room

4005
01:59:14.155 --> 01:59:16.571
to try and get stable again, do that.

4006
01:59:16.571 --> 01:59:19.435
And that is also a re-childing practice.

4007
01:59:19.435 --> 01:59:20.268
Mm-hmm.

4008
01:59:20.268 --> 01:59:22.249
You could say that's like dance, you know?

4009
01:59:22.249 --> 01:59:23.082
To start with.

4010
01:59:23.082 --> 01:59:24.883
And you're literally just trying to use the leg

4011
01:59:24.883 --> 01:59:26.508
to throw off you balance, you know?

4012
01:59:26.508 --> 01:59:28.695
And can you keep tuned into the foot

4013
01:59:28.695 --> 01:59:29.838
that keeps stabilizing you?

4014
01:59:29.838 --> 01:59:30.671
Right.

4015
01:59:30.671 --> 01:59:31.918
So the foot that's stabilizing you

4016
01:59:31.918 --> 01:59:33.701
is doing everything that it can

4017
01:59:33.701 --> 01:59:36.214
to just hold you upright, right?

4018
01:59:36.214 --> 01:59:37.047
Yeah.

4019
01:59:37.047 --> 01:59:37.918
So you're activating

4020
01:59:37.918 --> 01:59:39.690
all these weird pathways and muscles

4021
01:59:39.690 --> 01:59:41.828
that generally aren't triggered.

4022
01:59:41.828 --> 01:59:42.661
Yeah.

4023
01:59:42.661 --> 01:59:44.132
And I would go into, eventually,

4024
01:59:44.132 --> 01:59:45.479
the language being that

4025
01:59:45.479 --> 01:59:48.463
not seeing the foot as separate or outside of you.

4026
01:59:48.463 --> 01:59:50.130
So you are the foot.

4027
01:59:51.004 --> 01:59:51.991
You know?

4028
01:59:51.991 --> 01:59:53.518
You are the foot.

4029
01:59:53.518 --> 01:59:54.434
(Tony chuckles)

4030
01:59:54.434 --> 01:59:56.545
That's... I think that might be the title of this podcast.

4031
01:59:56.545 --> 01:59:58.636
(Tony and Rich laugh)

4032
01:59:58.636 --> 01:59:59.469
Yeah!

4033
01:59:59.469 --> 02:00:02.546
Some of this stuff we covered, well sort of,

4034
02:00:02.546 --> 02:00:03.813
for people that are new

4035
02:00:03.813 --> 02:00:05.616
and didn't listen to our first podcast,

4036
02:00:05.616 --> 02:00:06.977
we went into a lot of...

4037
02:00:06.977 --> 02:00:09.717
we spent a lot of time on your backstory in that one.

4038
02:00:09.717 --> 02:00:11.272
But one thing we did do was

4039
02:00:11.272 --> 02:00:13.575
after we recorded the podcast,

4040
02:00:13.575 --> 02:00:17.249
you took me through some technique work

4041
02:00:17.249 --> 02:00:20.666
and we kind of ran back and forth on my pool deck

4042
02:00:20.666 --> 02:00:22.571
and we filmed that and made a video of that-

4043
02:00:22.571 --> 02:00:23.404
Yeah.

4044
02:00:23.404 --> 02:00:24.911
...which we'll link up in the show notes there.

4045
02:00:24.911 --> 02:00:28.778
But a lot of that had to do with thinking about, you know,

4046
02:00:28.778 --> 02:00:30.144
lifting the foot,

4047
02:00:30.144 --> 02:00:31.477
lifting the leg,

4048
02:00:32.317 --> 02:00:33.150
as opposed to

4049
02:00:33.150 --> 02:00:36.267
overthinking where you're placing the foot. Right?

4050
02:00:36.267 --> 02:00:37.100
Yeah.

4051
02:00:37.100 --> 02:00:38.197
It's like a pop up.

4052
02:00:38.197 --> 02:00:39.386
Yeah, rather than a push down.

4053
02:00:39.386 --> 02:00:40.219
Exactly.

4054
02:00:40.219 --> 02:00:41.122
Yeah, yeah.

4055
02:00:41.122 --> 02:00:43.221
So maybe explain that a little bit.

4056
02:00:43.221 --> 02:00:44.775
Yeah and I've refined this somewhat now-

4057
02:00:44.775 --> 02:00:45.608
Oh, you have?

4058
02:00:45.608 --> 02:00:46.441
...'cause I've been...

4059
02:00:46.441 --> 02:00:47.274
yeah, what was that,

4060
02:00:47.274 --> 02:00:48.107
three years ago?

4061
02:00:48.107 --> 02:00:50.099
Three years ago, I've had a lot of miles of refinement

4062
02:00:50.099 --> 02:00:50.963
and really going deep into

4063
02:00:50.963 --> 02:00:53.282
kind of the you the softening

4064
02:00:53.282 --> 02:00:57.014
or I really like introducing the language of like

4065
02:00:57.014 --> 02:00:58.730
"leaving no trace."

4066
02:00:58.730 --> 02:00:59.563
You know?

4067
02:00:59.563 --> 02:01:02.853
Like leaving the least amount of impact on the Earth.

4068
02:01:02.853 --> 02:01:03.686
Right.

4069
02:01:03.686 --> 02:01:06.610
And part of that being like when your foot lands,

4070
02:01:06.610 --> 02:01:08.449
like how quickly can you lift it back up?

4071
02:01:08.449 --> 02:01:09.944
Like how gently can you...

4072
02:01:09.944 --> 02:01:11.027
and when your barefoot,

4073
02:01:11.027 --> 02:01:12.605
you're very hyper aware of how-

4074
02:01:12.605 --> 02:01:13.438
Yeah.

4075
02:01:13.438 --> 02:01:14.876
'Cause I have a very heavy foot.

4076
02:01:14.876 --> 02:01:16.720
And that was one of the things you were pointing out like,

4077
02:01:16.720 --> 02:01:18.909
man, you're just like pounding it,

4078
02:01:18.909 --> 02:01:20.082
like you're landing so hard.

4079
02:01:20.082 --> 02:01:20.915
Yeah.

4080
02:01:20.915 --> 02:01:24.245
Whereas trying to imagine you're almost like stealh-like,

4081
02:01:24.245 --> 02:01:27.220
so going back to that head, chest, pelvis

4082
02:01:27.220 --> 02:01:28.518
is to try and think about

4083
02:01:28.518 --> 02:01:30.307
keeping all those segments upright.

4084
02:01:30.307 --> 02:01:31.140
Visual field,

4085
02:01:31.140 --> 02:01:33.218
open up your visual field so you see everything

4086
02:01:33.218 --> 02:01:34.516
rather than hyper focus on one little, one area.

4087
02:01:34.516 --> 02:01:37.359
So if you imagine you are looking down right now

4088
02:01:37.359 --> 02:01:39.460
as if you're looking at your screen,

4089
02:01:39.460 --> 02:01:40.988
I can only see really to that glass.

4090
02:01:40.988 --> 02:01:42.595
The rest of it's a blur.

4091
02:01:42.595 --> 02:01:44.893
So when I'm running, I have that amount of time to react

4092
02:01:44.893 --> 02:01:46.393
by the time I get to the glass.

4093
02:01:46.393 --> 02:01:49.903
Whereas now I'm looking at the guitar over there, right?

4094
02:01:49.903 --> 02:01:51.111
I'm looking out the window almost.

4095
02:01:51.111 --> 02:01:52.383
I still see all of this.

4096
02:01:52.383 --> 02:01:54.060
I can see the glass, I can see everything.

4097
02:01:54.060 --> 02:01:55.936
So I have all this time to be really proactive

4098
02:01:55.936 --> 02:01:56.781
rather than reactive.

4099
02:01:56.781 --> 02:01:57.636
Right?

4100
02:01:57.636 --> 02:02:00.130
And my movement brain will make all those calculations.

4101
02:02:00.130 --> 02:02:02.316
That's how sophisticated it can go, right?

4102
02:02:02.316 --> 02:02:03.459
Visual field opens up,

4103
02:02:03.459 --> 02:02:05.828
nasal breathing will help you be more relaxed.

4104
02:02:05.828 --> 02:02:08.010
Visual field will help you be more relaxed

4105
02:02:08.010 --> 02:02:09.111
while you're out there running.

4106
02:02:09.111 --> 02:02:09.944
So that's less tension.

4107
02:02:09.944 --> 02:02:10.811
We become more efficient.

4108
02:02:10.811 --> 02:02:11.644
Right?

4109
02:02:11.644 --> 02:02:14.021
More efficient and also minimize the risk of injury

4110
02:02:14.021 --> 02:02:15.741
'cause we're not tense.

4111
02:02:15.741 --> 02:02:18.512
Keep the head up, the chest up, the pelvis stacked.

4112
02:02:18.512 --> 02:02:21.024
Think about getting your feet off the ground.

4113
02:02:21.024 --> 02:02:22.802
But it's not like you are just pulling them up

4114
02:02:22.802 --> 02:02:23.635
'cause that would mean

4115
02:02:23.635 --> 02:02:26.194
kicking your legs that back behind you almost.

4116
02:02:26.194 --> 02:02:28.958
Think almost like, they're-

4117
02:02:28.958 --> 02:02:30.262
You're riding a bike.

4118
02:02:30.262 --> 02:02:31.095
...they're like....

4119
02:02:31.095 --> 02:02:33.535
yeah, like a unicycle. so on a cyclic pattern.

4120
02:02:33.535 --> 02:02:35.406
And because there's a trajectory to the leg,

4121
02:02:35.406 --> 02:02:36.990
so by the time you've pulled it,

4122
02:02:36.990 --> 02:02:37.927
you've already moved forward.

4123
02:02:37.927 --> 02:02:38.773
You know?

4124
02:02:38.773 --> 02:02:40.941
So you have to think it's like an arc, right?

4125
02:02:40.941 --> 02:02:44.328
But only pull the leg as if it's going underneath your hip.

4126
02:02:44.328 --> 02:02:46.412
There's a great guy for this, his name's Nicholas Romanov.

4127
02:02:46.412 --> 02:02:48.075
He has the "Pose Method," right?

4128
02:02:48.075 --> 02:02:50.114
So all that pulling mechanics is in there.

4129
02:02:50.114 --> 02:02:50.994
So if you...

4130
02:02:50.994 --> 02:02:52.078
he did a great book

4131
02:02:52.078 --> 02:02:54.103
on the "Pose Method" of triathlon, right?

4132
02:02:54.103 --> 02:02:55.541
That's a brilliant book to unpack

4133
02:02:55.541 --> 02:02:56.734
'cause there's cycling in there,

4134
02:02:56.734 --> 02:02:57.567
swimming in there,

4135
02:02:57.567 --> 02:02:58.400
and there's running in there-

4136
02:02:58.400 --> 02:02:59.233
Hmmm.

4137
02:02:59.233 --> 02:03:00.931
...and it's in a much simpler format.

4138
02:03:00.931 --> 02:03:04.694
Whereas I've studied like a level three in Pose

4139
02:03:04.694 --> 02:03:07.494
and also one of the only six people on the planet

4140
02:03:07.494 --> 02:03:08.967
to teach pose movement.

4141
02:03:08.967 --> 02:03:09.800
Right?

4142
02:03:09.800 --> 02:03:10.998
But there's...

4143
02:03:10.998 --> 02:03:12.568
in the textbook version,

4144
02:03:12.568 --> 02:03:14.830
it can overwhelm a little bit.

4145
02:03:14.830 --> 02:03:16.922
It stimulates the intellectual mind.

4146
02:03:16.922 --> 02:03:18.727
But when you go out and experience it,

4147
02:03:18.727 --> 02:03:19.881
it's a little different.

4148
02:03:19.881 --> 02:03:21.367
So you play with just, yeah,

4149
02:03:21.367 --> 02:03:22.310
pulling the leg

4150
02:03:22.310 --> 02:03:25.532
and that gets the hamstrings involved.

4151
02:03:25.532 --> 02:03:27.339
Not so much about picking the knees up

4152
02:03:27.339 --> 02:03:28.669
and driving through the knees.

4153
02:03:28.669 --> 02:03:30.060
You'll think about your hamstrings.

4154
02:03:30.060 --> 02:03:31.319
And the hamstrings are huge pulling muscles.

4155
02:03:31.319 --> 02:03:33.040
So if you think of the bicep,

4156
02:03:33.040 --> 02:03:34.668
if you pull your bicep right now

4157
02:03:34.668 --> 02:03:36.678
and you flex your arm to do that,

4158
02:03:36.678 --> 02:03:37.546
that's a bicep.

4159
02:03:37.546 --> 02:03:39.789
Well you have a bicep for (indistinct) as well.

4160
02:03:39.789 --> 02:03:40.899
It's like a huge hamstring-

4161
02:03:40.899 --> 02:03:41.732
Mm-hmm.

4162
02:03:41.732 --> 02:03:42.565
...that we pull.

4163
02:03:42.565 --> 02:03:43.936
So you get used to the pulling cycles

4164
02:03:43.936 --> 02:03:45.808
and then when you're going over terrain,

4165
02:03:45.808 --> 02:03:48.251
rather than even any thought of putting your foot down,

4166
02:03:48.251 --> 02:03:49.084
forget that.

4167
02:03:49.084 --> 02:03:51.037
Just think about getting off the ground.

4168
02:03:51.037 --> 02:03:52.639
And the lighter you get the better

4169
02:03:52.639 --> 02:03:55.418
because it just gets softer and softer and softer.

4170
02:03:55.418 --> 02:03:56.943
And that's in that pulling cycle,

4171
02:03:56.943 --> 02:03:59.048
it's refining the pulling cycle.

4172
02:03:59.048 --> 02:04:00.610
There is a cadence to that

4173
02:04:00.610 --> 02:04:01.686
where we have to observe

4174
02:04:01.686 --> 02:04:05.336
somewhere between 176 and 182 beats per minute.

4175
02:04:05.336 --> 02:04:06.970
And I often work with breath with this.

4176
02:04:06.970 --> 02:04:08.537
I kind of breathe in tempos now.

4177
02:04:08.537 --> 02:04:09.867
I'm working on refining that.

4178
02:04:09.867 --> 02:04:10.700
So there's

4179
02:04:10.700 --> 02:04:11.763
(Tony inhales four times )

4180
02:04:11.763 --> 02:04:12.777
four little intakes

4181
02:04:12.777 --> 02:04:13.848
and then

4182
02:04:13.848 --> 02:04:14.681
(Tony exhales four times)

4183
02:04:14.681 --> 02:04:15.514
four outtakes.

4184
02:04:15.514 --> 02:04:16.347
Mm-hmm.

4185
02:04:16.347 --> 02:04:17.180
Or,

4186
02:04:17.180 --> 02:04:19.932
(Tony inhales and exhales steadily and repeatedly)

4187
02:04:19.932 --> 02:04:20.901
And I match that to my tempo pulling.

4188
02:04:20.901 --> 02:04:21.734
Mmm.

4189
02:04:21.734 --> 02:04:22.567
So it's like

4190
02:04:22.567 --> 02:04:23.400
pull, pull, pull, pull,

4191
02:04:23.400 --> 02:04:24.233
pull, pull, pull, pull.

4192
02:04:24.233 --> 02:04:25.066
And-

4193
02:04:25.066 --> 02:04:26.299
So the breath is totally in sync

4194
02:04:26.299 --> 02:04:27.502
with your running cadence.

4195
02:04:27.502 --> 02:04:28.335
Yeah.

4196
02:04:28.335 --> 02:04:29.168
Yeah.

4197
02:04:29.168 --> 02:04:30.707
And then you don't have to get so...

4198
02:04:30.707 --> 02:04:32.700
more often than not we're over-breathing.

4199
02:04:32.700 --> 02:04:34.928
So it's like, you don't need to breathe that hard.

4200
02:04:34.928 --> 02:04:35.761
Right?

4201
02:04:35.761 --> 02:04:38.002
You can soften the breath.

4202
02:04:38.002 --> 02:04:39.101
But I really feel that, yeah,

4203
02:04:39.101 --> 02:04:40.819
softening the knees a little bit,

4204
02:04:40.819 --> 02:04:43.306
thinking about being upright, really tall.

4205
02:04:43.306 --> 02:04:45.292
I have this language of leading with the heart

4206
02:04:45.292 --> 02:04:46.277
and then your feet follow.

4207
02:04:46.277 --> 02:04:47.906
So by that, I mean your actual sternum,

4208
02:04:47.906 --> 02:04:49.236
your chest plate,

4209
02:04:49.236 --> 02:04:51.560
think about that actually being a lead segment.

4210
02:04:51.560 --> 02:04:53.266
So it's prowed of your face.

4211
02:04:53.266 --> 02:04:55.827
It's actually further forward than your actual head.

4212
02:04:55.827 --> 02:04:58.583
And that huge weight being your ribcage and your pelvis,

4213
02:04:58.583 --> 02:05:00.120
you'll be amazed at how we can-

4214
02:05:00.120 --> 02:05:01.371
Mm-hmm.

4215
02:05:01.371 --> 02:05:03.373
...almost think like a piece of cellophane

4216
02:05:03.373 --> 02:05:04.206
in front of you.

4217
02:05:04.206 --> 02:05:05.922
Like a huge piece of clING film like that.

4218
02:05:05.922 --> 02:05:07.892
And you're literally falling into that with your chest-

4219
02:05:07.892 --> 02:05:08.725
Mm-hmm.

4220
02:05:08.725 --> 02:05:09.884
... and just keep picking your feet up

4221
02:05:09.884 --> 02:05:11.509
and those feet will stay underneath you.

4222
02:05:11.509 --> 02:05:13.414
And what I'm discovering out on that path,

4223
02:05:13.414 --> 02:05:15.399
the South West Coastal Path,

4224
02:05:15.399 --> 02:05:16.962
by keeping the feet underneath you,

4225
02:05:16.962 --> 02:05:18.411
they're just shock absorbers.

4226
02:05:18.411 --> 02:05:19.332
It's like having

4227
02:05:19.332 --> 02:05:21.804
these incredible shock absorbers underneath you,

4228
02:05:21.804 --> 02:05:22.882
which you can't find

4229
02:05:22.882 --> 02:05:25.000
when you're having this huge pendulum effect

4230
02:05:25.000 --> 02:05:26.059
and landing on your heel.

4231
02:05:26.059 --> 02:05:28.683
There's huge kind of forces you're dealing with there,

4232
02:05:28.683 --> 02:05:30.193
like landing on a rigid pin,

4233
02:05:30.193 --> 02:05:31.279
you know,

4234
02:05:31.279 --> 02:05:33.206
whereas allow yourself to land underneath you

4235
02:05:33.206 --> 02:05:34.607
and it's much softer

4236
02:05:34.607 --> 02:05:37.618
and that cyclic action will come through with it.

4237
02:05:37.618 --> 02:05:39.468
That video was good though. We covered a fair bit of that.

4238
02:05:39.468 --> 02:05:40.470
I think that's worth going back to.

4239
02:05:40.470 --> 02:05:41.303
I think so.

4240
02:05:41.303 --> 02:05:43.165
I mean, what you just described is a little bit different

4241
02:05:43.165 --> 02:05:44.107
'cause, I mean,

4242
02:05:44.107 --> 02:05:46.411
I haven't gone back and watched it in a very long time,

4243
02:05:46.411 --> 02:05:49.250
but I do recall something about like,

4244
02:05:49.250 --> 02:05:50.083
you know,

4245
02:05:50.083 --> 02:05:50.995
the lifting part.

4246
02:05:50.995 --> 02:05:51.828
Yeah.

4247
02:05:51.828 --> 02:05:53.459
So it sounds like, you know, in a nuanced way,

4248
02:05:53.459 --> 02:05:55.278
you're letting go of some of that

4249
02:05:55.278 --> 02:05:58.861
and focused on just the light touch of the feet and...

4250
02:05:58.861 --> 02:06:02.128
I mean I do remember the leading with the heart part.

4251
02:06:02.128 --> 02:06:02.961
Yeah.

4252
02:06:02.961 --> 02:06:04.826
And the sort of leaning forward aspect of it.

4253
02:06:04.826 --> 02:06:05.659
Yeah.

4254
02:06:05.659 --> 02:06:06.524
And for people that are listening to this,

4255
02:06:06.524 --> 02:06:07.968
and you're new to Tony

4256
02:06:07.968 --> 02:06:09.009
and you're thinking like,

4257
02:06:09.009 --> 02:06:09.842
well you know,

4258
02:06:09.842 --> 02:06:12.882
the whole barefoot thing is, you know, bollocks, you know?

4259
02:06:12.882 --> 02:06:13.715
(Rich laughs)

4260
02:06:13.715 --> 02:06:14.641
I mean like,

4261
02:06:14.641 --> 02:06:17.735
we went out and ran in Malibu State Park,

4262
02:06:17.735 --> 02:06:18.607
Malibu Creek State Park,

4263
02:06:18.607 --> 02:06:21.854
running over all kinds of gravel,

4264
02:06:21.854 --> 02:06:22.687
small boulders,

4265
02:06:22.687 --> 02:06:24.060
you know-

4266
02:06:24.060 --> 02:06:25.095
Roots.

4267
02:06:25.095 --> 02:06:26.134
...all kinds of thorns and roots.

4268
02:06:26.134 --> 02:06:29.036
And it was a, you know, varied terrain.

4269
02:06:29.036 --> 02:06:31.639
I couldn't imagine doing that barefoot.

4270
02:06:31.639 --> 02:06:35.247
And it was a marvel to like, watch you, you know,

4271
02:06:35.247 --> 02:06:36.935
dance over that terrain

4272
02:06:36.935 --> 02:06:37.891
and crush it

4273
02:06:37.891 --> 02:06:40.253
and do it joyfully.

4274
02:06:40.253 --> 02:06:41.086
Not like,

4275
02:06:41.086 --> 02:06:43.636
"oh, this is hurting, but I'm gonna like get through it."

4276
02:06:43.636 --> 02:06:44.764
And then watching the

4277
02:06:44.764 --> 02:06:47.436
"One Man, Two Feet, Three Peaks" documentary,

4278
02:06:47.436 --> 02:06:48.269
you know,

4279
02:06:48.269 --> 02:06:50.862
some of those ascents and descents,

4280
02:06:50.862 --> 02:06:53.825
I mean under like wet, sharp boulders,

4281
02:06:53.825 --> 02:06:54.658
you know,

4282
02:06:54.658 --> 02:06:57.084
a lot of like technical...

4283
02:06:57.084 --> 02:06:59.387
like much more technical as you,

4284
02:06:59.387 --> 02:07:00.220
you know,

4285
02:07:00.220 --> 02:07:01.483
get above the treeline

4286
02:07:01.483 --> 02:07:03.178
and are approximating these summits.

4287
02:07:03.178 --> 02:07:04.011
Mmm.

4288
02:07:04.011 --> 02:07:04.876
And to see you do that barefoot,

4289
02:07:04.876 --> 02:07:06.648
like it's really quite something.

4290
02:07:06.648 --> 02:07:07.481
Well,

4291
02:07:07.481 --> 02:07:08.904
I think there's something to observe in that as well.

4292
02:07:08.904 --> 02:07:09.763
Like on the first...

4293
02:07:09.763 --> 02:07:11.086
I went up the day before as well.

4294
02:07:11.086 --> 02:07:13.845
I did like a practice kind of just to (indistinct)

4295
02:07:13.845 --> 02:07:14.678
Mm-hmm.

4296
02:07:14.678 --> 02:07:15.642
...said I'm just gonna go and run up there

4297
02:07:15.642 --> 02:07:16.565
just to see how it feels.

4298
02:07:16.565 --> 02:07:17.613
So I did...

4299
02:07:17.613 --> 02:07:19.176
it was like, I did the four peaks really.

4300
02:07:19.176 --> 02:07:20.140
So I did-

4301
02:07:20.140 --> 02:07:20.973
Yeah.

4302
02:07:20.973 --> 02:07:21.806
...but it had snowed-

4303
02:07:21.806 --> 02:07:22.639
Yeah, you set out in the...

4304
02:07:22.639 --> 02:07:23.477
like you went out and kind of did it the day before,

4305
02:07:23.477 --> 02:07:24.310
like I was like,

4306
02:07:24.310 --> 02:07:25.143
only you would do that.

4307
02:07:25.143 --> 02:07:26.589
So it did snow though the day before

4308
02:07:26.589 --> 02:07:29.072
and it was like quick up and down.

4309
02:07:29.072 --> 02:07:30.037
147 I think.

4310
02:07:30.037 --> 02:07:31.002
And-

4311
02:07:31.002 --> 02:07:33.896
It's like a thousand meters plus-

4312
02:07:33.896 --> 02:07:35.670
It's 2000 something, that one.

4313
02:07:35.670 --> 02:07:36.503
...in elevation.

4314
02:07:36.503 --> 02:07:37.336
Oh, is it?

4315
02:07:37.336 --> 02:07:38.169
And then...

4316
02:07:38.169 --> 02:07:41.374
so that's the first ascent and then down.

4317
02:07:41.374 --> 02:07:43.305
Which I was...

4318
02:07:43.305 --> 02:07:46.154
the camera crew, right on the day going off,

4319
02:07:46.154 --> 02:07:46.987
they were just like,

4320
02:07:46.987 --> 02:07:48.948
"we had no idea he was gonna tackle it like this."

4321
02:07:48.948 --> 02:07:49.802
So they then had to

4322
02:07:49.802 --> 02:07:51.614
position like another guy at midway points,

4323
02:07:51.614 --> 02:07:53.327
so they could capture the content

4324
02:07:53.327 --> 02:07:55.031
as they had aspirations-

4325
02:07:55.031 --> 02:07:57.242
they thought they were going to run with me, I think.

4326
02:07:57.242 --> 02:07:59.034
And then... so tour up.

4327
02:07:59.034 --> 02:08:01.672
And then on the way down of that first day,

4328
02:08:01.672 --> 02:08:03.086
it was really busy.

4329
02:08:03.086 --> 02:08:05.722
And so I had to swerve to miss some people.

4330
02:08:05.722 --> 02:08:07.031
And even if you were in footwear,

4331
02:08:07.031 --> 02:08:08.257
this would've happened, right?

4332
02:08:08.257 --> 02:08:10.466
I just hit something, really full on.

4333
02:08:10.466 --> 02:08:11.728
Broke a toe.

4334
02:08:11.728 --> 02:08:12.949
And then,

4335
02:08:12.949 --> 02:08:14.225
it wasn't until we returned

4336
02:08:14.225 --> 02:08:16.064
back to where base camp was at that moment

4337
02:08:16.064 --> 02:08:18.199
where I could then get some

4338
02:08:18.199 --> 02:08:19.032
plant-based gains on board at that point.

4339
02:08:19.032 --> 02:08:21.883
And then just get off again, back out.

4340
02:08:21.883 --> 02:08:26.586
I was just like, "ah, okay, feet don't feel that great."

4341
02:08:26.586 --> 02:08:28.358
And then we noticed, I had a...

4342
02:08:28.358 --> 02:08:30.819
there was a blood blister that had formed,

4343
02:08:30.819 --> 02:08:33.312
so I'd obviously already made adjustments getting down

4344
02:08:33.312 --> 02:08:34.145
because I'd broken a toe.

4345
02:08:34.145 --> 02:08:34.978
Mm-hmm.

4346
02:08:34.978 --> 02:08:36.121
The adrenaline and everything

4347
02:08:36.121 --> 02:08:36.954
of running down on that first day,

4348
02:08:36.954 --> 02:08:39.187
didn't really pay much attention to it.

4349
02:08:39.187 --> 02:08:42.551
And then went out and did two marathons after that.

4350
02:08:42.551 --> 02:08:44.516
It was like a 52-miler after that

4351
02:08:44.516 --> 02:08:46.109
and then returned back to base camp.

4352
02:08:46.109 --> 02:08:47.880
And it was really evident like,

4353
02:08:47.880 --> 02:08:51.350
you could even see, it was like a white marker,

4354
02:08:51.350 --> 02:08:53.151
like I'd been wearing a ring around my toe

4355
02:08:53.151 --> 02:08:53.984
and then a purple end to it

4356
02:08:53.984 --> 02:08:55.541
and a purple part behind it.

4357
02:08:55.541 --> 02:08:58.028
And then went on to the next one, the next one.

4358
02:08:58.028 --> 02:09:00.122
Then Scafell, Scafell was next.

4359
02:09:00.122 --> 02:09:00.955
That's like...

4360
02:09:00.955 --> 02:09:02.846
so Snowdon is the highest Welsh peak,

4361
02:09:02.846 --> 02:09:04.945
and then the English highest peak is Scafell,

4362
02:09:04.945 --> 02:09:08.420
and then there's Ben Nevis to finish, which is Scotland.

4363
02:09:08.420 --> 02:09:10.161
And the English one, Scafell,

4364
02:09:10.161 --> 02:09:11.155
it's like boulder fields,

4365
02:09:11.155 --> 02:09:13.458
like huge, sharp boulders everywhere.

4366
02:09:13.458 --> 02:09:16.649
And I, from that first one, I think just...

4367
02:09:16.649 --> 02:09:19.096
I lost a lot of the elasticity

4368
02:09:19.096 --> 02:09:21.270
in that there's, as I said,

4369
02:09:21.270 --> 02:09:25.482
I think there's a 7,400 elevation on the first day

4370
02:09:25.482 --> 02:09:27.689
that then crept into the next two days

4371
02:09:27.689 --> 02:09:31.219
and getting the mileage in and then doing two marathons,

4372
02:09:31.219 --> 02:09:33.999
Two marathons and then onto the next mountain.

4373
02:09:33.999 --> 02:09:34.832
Mm-hmm.

4374
02:09:34.832 --> 02:09:36.273
There was rigidity in those legs

4375
02:09:36.273 --> 02:09:37.477
and there was stiffness in the feet.

4376
02:09:37.477 --> 02:09:38.912
So it then became, like,

4377
02:09:38.912 --> 02:09:41.318
when people say about taking up barefoot,

4378
02:09:41.318 --> 02:09:43.412
that was the example I could give.

4379
02:09:43.412 --> 02:09:45.349
It's like I'd lost the compliance

4380
02:09:45.349 --> 02:09:46.454
and my own compliance

4381
02:09:46.454 --> 02:09:47.529
and my own softness

4382
02:09:47.529 --> 02:09:49.389
and my own elasticity.

4383
02:09:49.389 --> 02:09:51.799
And I'd become hitting hard on hard.

4384
02:09:51.799 --> 02:09:52.837
So it suddenly became like,

4385
02:09:52.837 --> 02:09:53.673
"ooh!"

4386
02:09:53.673 --> 02:09:55.689
Every rock hurt on that next one.

4387
02:09:55.689 --> 02:09:57.690
And then after Scafell was out the way,

4388
02:09:57.690 --> 02:09:59.456
it was almost like the midpoint.

4389
02:09:59.456 --> 02:10:00.658
It was like the one in the middle

4390
02:10:00.658 --> 02:10:02.367
that had to be the one that cracked Tony.

4391
02:10:02.367 --> 02:10:04.498
And it was... and I had to endure that.

4392
02:10:04.498 --> 02:10:05.331
I had to go through it.

4393
02:10:05.331 --> 02:10:06.164
It was almost,

4394
02:10:06.164 --> 02:10:07.863
that was the rite of passage on that event.

4395
02:10:07.863 --> 02:10:10.930
And then I found that elasticity back in...

4396
02:10:10.930 --> 02:10:12.936
on the beginning of Ben Nevis.

4397
02:10:12.936 --> 02:10:14.220
And then Ben Nevis,

4398
02:10:14.220 --> 02:10:15.861
we had severe weather came in.

4399
02:10:15.861 --> 02:10:16.694
Mm-hmm.

4400
02:10:16.694 --> 02:10:19.221
My intention with that was to...

4401
02:10:19.221 --> 02:10:20.077
I could have done it...

4402
02:10:20.077 --> 02:10:22.165
Basically, we could have broke it,

4403
02:10:22.165 --> 02:10:22.998
the fastest known time,

4404
02:10:22.998 --> 02:10:24.662
we could have done it a day earlier.

4405
02:10:24.662 --> 02:10:28.178
But, at base camp where we were in Scotland,

4406
02:10:28.178 --> 02:10:29.011
we arrived back

4407
02:10:29.011 --> 02:10:30.514
and the film crew were there.

4408
02:10:30.514 --> 02:10:31.347
And they're like,

4409
02:10:31.347 --> 02:10:32.834
"Tony, how's that and what's happening tomorrow?"

4410
02:10:32.834 --> 02:10:33.667
And I said,

4411
02:10:33.667 --> 02:10:35.293
"oh, you know, I think we're gonna...

4412
02:10:35.293 --> 02:10:36.250
I think we should go up with...

4413
02:10:36.250 --> 02:10:37.402
let's not go back out now.

4414
02:10:37.402 --> 02:10:38.324
What we'll do is...

4415
02:10:38.324 --> 02:10:40.235
I wanna go out with Katarina and the kids

4416
02:10:40.235 --> 02:10:41.678
and we go tomorrow morning.

4417
02:10:41.678 --> 02:10:42.878
'Cause I think it'd be much nicer

4418
02:10:42.878 --> 02:10:44.184
to make that memory with the kids."

4419
02:10:44.184 --> 02:10:45.283
And he said, "well, what about the record?"

4420
02:10:45.283 --> 02:10:46.116
And I was like, "oh, screw the record.

4421
02:10:46.116 --> 02:10:47.576
Let's just... it'd be amazing to have that memory.

4422
02:10:47.576 --> 02:10:50.084
Let's go up with Katarina and the kids."

4423
02:10:50.084 --> 02:10:51.124
And so we left it.

4424
02:10:51.124 --> 02:10:53.250
And so instead of going out that day,

4425
02:10:53.250 --> 02:10:55.073
it was like the next day.

4426
02:10:55.073 --> 02:10:57.171
And yet, severe weather came in.

4427
02:10:57.171 --> 02:10:58.004
So we then had,

4428
02:10:58.004 --> 02:10:59.098
I think it was -4°

4429
02:10:59.098 --> 02:11:01.646
and 50 mile per hour winds that came in.

4430
02:11:01.646 --> 02:11:03.581
So there was no Katarina and the kids going out that day.

4431
02:11:03.581 --> 02:11:04.414
Mm-hmm.

4432
02:11:04.414 --> 02:11:05.604
But it did mean enduring that

4433
02:11:05.604 --> 02:11:06.872
when we were up there.

4434
02:11:06.872 --> 02:11:09.319
And, again, that cold gets in

4435
02:11:09.319 --> 02:11:11.569
and you then create, again,

4436
02:11:12.721 --> 02:11:15.470
what feels like hard on hard with the feet.

4437
02:11:15.470 --> 02:11:16.303
Yeah.

4438
02:11:16.303 --> 02:11:17.864
So as romantic as the idea is,

4439
02:11:17.864 --> 02:11:18.983
there's that...

4440
02:11:18.983 --> 02:11:23.098
you can see why through humanity we developed skins

4441
02:11:23.098 --> 02:11:25.557
and we developed something

4442
02:11:25.557 --> 02:11:27.522
that would protect us from those environments.

4443
02:11:27.522 --> 02:11:28.355
Yeah.

4444
02:11:28.355 --> 02:11:29.508
And that's just a layer, back then.

4445
02:11:29.508 --> 02:11:30.624
It wasn't aesthetics

4446
02:11:30.624 --> 02:11:32.026
and it wasn't cushioning

4447
02:11:32.026 --> 02:11:33.819
and it wasn't of an unnatural shape.

4448
02:11:33.819 --> 02:11:37.385
It was something that was from the earth, let's say.

4449
02:11:37.385 --> 02:11:38.218
Right?

4450
02:11:38.218 --> 02:11:39.117
Right.

4451
02:11:39.117 --> 02:11:40.340
And the lesson, from that,

4452
02:11:40.340 --> 02:11:41.737
for the average person

4453
02:11:41.737 --> 02:11:44.893
who's contemplating getting into the barefoot thing

4454
02:11:44.893 --> 02:11:45.893
is patience.

4455
02:11:47.198 --> 02:11:48.522
Yeah, 100%.

4456
02:11:48.522 --> 02:11:49.355
Yeah.

4457
02:11:49.355 --> 02:11:51.151
And I think that the kind of learning curve,

4458
02:11:51.151 --> 02:11:54.398
the arc towards competency is much longer

4459
02:11:54.398 --> 02:11:56.022
than people realize.

4460
02:11:56.022 --> 02:11:57.574
100%. Yeah.

4461
02:11:57.574 --> 02:11:58.652
I mean, I've found myself...

4462
02:11:58.652 --> 02:12:01.201
like I get enamored with it and I do it for a while

4463
02:12:01.201 --> 02:12:04.348
and then I end up, you know, defaulting back.

4464
02:12:04.348 --> 02:12:06.100
And I don't think I've ever stuck with it

4465
02:12:06.100 --> 02:12:10.921
long enough to really have the dividends pay off.

4466
02:12:10.921 --> 02:12:11.754
Yeah.

4467
02:12:11.754 --> 02:12:12.587
Of being...

4468
02:12:12.587 --> 02:12:14.636
'cause I haven't been fully invested in it.

4469
02:12:14.636 --> 02:12:15.802
Again, I would go into, you know,

4470
02:12:15.802 --> 02:12:19.209
what is it that we're trying to achieve from it?

4471
02:12:19.209 --> 02:12:20.375
And, you know, what's the purpose

4472
02:12:20.375 --> 02:12:22.313
and why am I doing it?

4473
02:12:22.313 --> 02:12:25.651
And for me now, it's on that...

4474
02:12:25.651 --> 02:12:28.677
it's really beyond the physiological,

4475
02:12:28.677 --> 02:12:29.859
as I said earlier,

4476
02:12:29.859 --> 02:12:31.480
I think there's that whole neuroplasticity there,

4477
02:12:31.480 --> 02:12:33.100
there's the microbiome that's involved in it

4478
02:12:33.100 --> 02:12:36.644
and just interacting with different environments that,

4479
02:12:36.644 --> 02:12:37.477
again,

4480
02:12:37.477 --> 02:12:38.865
we can have that conversation that we are nature

4481
02:12:38.865 --> 02:12:39.963
not separate of it, right?

4482
02:12:39.963 --> 02:12:40.796
Right.

4483
02:12:40.796 --> 02:12:41.629
Rather than this,

4484
02:12:41.629 --> 02:12:43.025
I'm wearing a huge piece of rubber and I'm disconnected.

4485
02:12:43.025 --> 02:12:45.505
I'm gonna go around stamping my print on the earth, right?

4486
02:12:45.505 --> 02:12:46.338
It's like,

4487
02:12:46.338 --> 02:12:48.165
"oh, okay, how can I be 'leave no trace?'"

4488
02:12:48.165 --> 02:12:49.475
It's the bigger picture kind of that, isn't it?

4489
02:12:49.475 --> 02:12:50.308
"Leave no trace;"

4490
02:12:50.308 --> 02:12:51.788
at least leave the least amount of impact-

4491
02:12:51.788 --> 02:12:52.621
Mmm.

4492
02:12:52.621 --> 02:12:53.454
...on the planet, right?

4493
02:12:53.454 --> 02:12:54.287
Not just when you're running.

4494
02:12:54.287 --> 02:12:55.819
But yeah, I just think it's...

4495
02:12:55.819 --> 02:12:59.639
it's that relationship that we can have through running,

4496
02:12:59.639 --> 02:13:00.472
you know?

4497
02:13:00.472 --> 02:13:02.032
But yeah, it ticks multiple boxes.

4498
02:13:02.032 --> 02:13:03.052
But I guess

4499
02:13:03.052 --> 02:13:05.927
the more and more gadgetry we involve ourselves in

4500
02:13:05.927 --> 02:13:08.302
the more and more, you know,

4501
02:13:08.302 --> 02:13:10.940
heart rate monitors and phones and G.P.S.,

4502
02:13:10.940 --> 02:13:14.118
how much of that, how much are we losing?

4503
02:13:14.118 --> 02:13:14.951
Right?

4504
02:13:14.951 --> 02:13:15.813
Yeah.

4505
02:13:15.813 --> 02:13:17.739
You know, and you might start off with just,

4506
02:13:17.739 --> 02:13:19.898
even if it means a walk in the park,

4507
02:13:19.898 --> 02:13:21.112
just go and investigate that.

4508
02:13:21.112 --> 02:13:22.165
How's it feel to take your shoes off?

4509
02:13:22.165 --> 02:13:22.998
You know,

4510
02:13:22.998 --> 02:13:25.409
there's that school group I took out

4511
02:13:25.409 --> 02:13:27.756
and they gave us the real...

4512
02:13:27.756 --> 02:13:30.831
you know, because there's no adult mask

4513
02:13:30.831 --> 02:13:33.678
and all that, "oh, I wonder what people think of me."

4514
02:13:33.678 --> 02:13:36.032
But we... they often invited me to take kids out

4515
02:13:36.032 --> 02:13:37.834
to this place called Virginia Water.

4516
02:13:37.834 --> 02:13:40.143
It's very near to where we lived in Windsor.

4517
02:13:40.143 --> 02:13:41.313
They're called "Busy Buttons."

4518
02:13:41.313 --> 02:13:45.065
They're like an un-schooling group, creative group.

4519
02:13:45.065 --> 02:13:47.410
"Tony, do you think you can take the kids out re-wilding?"

4520
02:13:47.410 --> 02:13:49.509
I said, "oh, kids don't need re-wilding."

4521
02:13:49.509 --> 02:13:50.564
"But, you know, we'd really like you to-"

4522
02:13:50.564 --> 02:13:51.397
"ah okay, let's go."

4523
02:13:51.397 --> 02:13:52.230
(Rich laughs)

4524
02:13:52.230 --> 02:13:53.647
And we went on this coach,

4525
02:13:53.647 --> 02:13:56.214
and we arrive at this place in Virginia Water

4526
02:13:56.214 --> 02:13:57.886
and all the kids get off and they're super excited.

4527
02:13:57.886 --> 02:13:59.753
And we go and we walk out into this...

4528
02:13:59.753 --> 02:14:02.423
over a bridge and into a meadow.

4529
02:14:02.423 --> 02:14:04.520
And firstly I sit all the kids around

4530
02:14:04.520 --> 02:14:06.097
and get them all in a circle and I say,

4531
02:14:06.097 --> 02:14:07.982
"okay, just sit and tune in for a bit."

4532
02:14:07.982 --> 02:14:09.874
I even brought my adult coaching into that stage.

4533
02:14:09.874 --> 02:14:11.786
"Just have a think, what can you hear?"

4534
02:14:11.786 --> 02:14:13.878
You know, "if you close your eyes, really tune in,

4535
02:14:13.878 --> 02:14:14.711
what can you hear?"

4536
02:14:14.711 --> 02:14:15.635
You know, and us,

4537
02:14:15.635 --> 02:14:17.463
we'd be looking for the bumblebee

4538
02:14:17.463 --> 02:14:19.406
or the bird or the sound of a leaf

4539
02:14:19.406 --> 02:14:21.229
or the blade of a grass.

4540
02:14:21.229 --> 02:14:23.870
And they were like "a lion, I can hear a lion!"

4541
02:14:23.870 --> 02:14:24.703
(Rich and Tony laugh)

4542
02:14:24.703 --> 02:14:25.536
You know?

4543
02:14:25.536 --> 02:14:26.369
Yeah.

4544
02:14:26.369 --> 02:14:27.397
And it's like, this is brilliant.

4545
02:14:27.397 --> 02:14:28.230
That imagination.

4546
02:14:28.230 --> 02:14:29.063
Yeah, that's what where it's at. Right?

4547
02:14:29.063 --> 02:14:29.896
Right.

4548
02:14:29.896 --> 02:14:31.117
So then the next thing's like, okay,

4549
02:14:31.117 --> 02:14:32.710
